{:edb/db-before-t 1052 :edb/db-after-t 1053 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534365 50 #inst "2026-10-06T17:12:00.261Z" 13194139534365 true] [17592186157259 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284217 13194139534365 true] [17592186157259 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284224 13194139534365 true] [17592186157260 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284231 13194139534365 true] [17592186157260 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284238 13194139534365 true] [17592186157260 1020 #edb/ref 17592186284243 13194139534365 true] [17592186284217 1017 #edb/ref 17592186284218 13194139534365 true] [17592186284217 1089 "q-102975" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284217 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534365 true] [17592186284217 1100 "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x+5)-2\\le 0.$" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284217 1113 #uuid "9e98cc8a-a07a-5c51-9aa1-e06510c614aa" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284217 1145 "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x+5)-2 & \\le 0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x+5) & \\le 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x+5)} & \\le {e}^{2} \\\\ 3x+5 & \\le {e}^{2} \\\\ 3x & \\le {e}^{2}-5 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{{e}^{2}-5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n3x+5>0\\,⇒\\,x>-\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le \\frac{{e}^{2}-5}{3},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,\\frac{{e}^{2}-5}{3}].$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>-\\frac{5}{3},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\frac{5}{3},\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{{e}^{2}-5}{3}]\\cap (-\\frac{5}{3},\\infty )=(-\\frac{5}{3},\\frac{{e}^{2}-5}{3}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (-\\frac{5}{3},\\frac{{e}^{2}-5}{3}],$ which can also be written as $-\\frac{5}{3}<x\\le \\frac{{e}^{2}-5}{3}.$" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284218 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284219 13194139534365 true] [17592186284218 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284220 13194139534365 true] [17592186284218 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284221 13194139534365 true] [17592186284218 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284222 13194139534365 true] [17592186284218 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284223 13194139534365 true] [17592186284218 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534365 true] [17592186284218 1041 "selection" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284218 1104 #edb/ref 17592186284222 13194139534365 true] [17592186284218 1110 #uuid "6cc7202c-f285-5f70-9dce-26b35116840b" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284219 1034 "x\\le \\frac{{e}^{2}-5}{3}" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284219 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534365 true] [17592186284219 1111 #uuid "9611562c-7b6a-5fd7-b4a4-8b909569962f" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284220 1034 "x>-\\frac{5}{3}" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284220 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534365 true] [17592186284220 1111 #uuid "28f5f1a5-f068-5698-8912-f4cf6b923f77" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284221 1034 "1\\le x<\\frac{5}{3}" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284221 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534365 true] [17592186284221 1111 #uuid "a22b9d80-841f-5778-84d6-4f7c8885f87a" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284222 1034 "-\\frac{5}{3}<x\\le \\frac{{e}^{2}-5}{3}" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284222 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534365 true] [17592186284222 1111 #uuid "7618b474-e6f0-5c09-8fc1-46f7827e714b" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284223 1034 "0\\le x\\le \\frac{{e}^{2}-5}{3}" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284223 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534365 true] [17592186284223 1111 #uuid "2f04df1c-d3b2-5364-bb89-a2e0e96377dc" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284224 1017 #edb/ref 17592186284225 13194139534365 true] [17592186284224 1089 "q-102977" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284224 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534365 true] [17592186284224 1100 "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-x)-1\\ge 0.$" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284224 1113 #uuid "b3da04af-bb57-590e-a310-51cf6887be50" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284224 1145 "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-x)-1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-x) & \\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-x)} & \\ge {e}^{1} \\\\ 4-x & \\ge e \\\\ -x & \\ge e-4 \\\\ x & \\le 4-e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n4-x>0\\,⇒\\,x<4\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le 4-e,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,4-e].$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x<4,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,4).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,4-e]\\cap (-\\infty ,4)=(-\\infty ,4-e]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,4-e],$ which can also be written as $x\\le 4-e.$" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284225 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284226 13194139534365 true] [17592186284225 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284227 13194139534365 true] [17592186284225 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284228 13194139534365 true] [17592186284225 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284229 13194139534365 true] [17592186284225 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284230 13194139534365 true] [17592186284225 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534365 true] [17592186284225 1041 "selection" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284225 1104 #edb/ref 17592186284229 13194139534365 true] [17592186284225 1110 #uuid "fbf3f98f-2491-512f-b9cc-4a8adf2e0ef4" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284226 1034 "4-e\\le x<4" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284226 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534365 true] [17592186284226 1111 #uuid "262126df-a114-500d-87d4-491e46c43b95" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284227 1034 "x<4" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284227 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534365 true] [17592186284227 1111 #uuid "0fffda1e-cf30-52eb-8f5b-b68537b0b2e1" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284228 1034 "1\\le x\\le 4-e" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284228 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534365 true] [17592186284228 1111 #uuid "25a16f5d-267f-55da-a0eb-959002ab150c" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284229 1034 "x\\le 4-e" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284229 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534365 true] [17592186284229 1111 #uuid "2e30063e-67ec-5a13-9f9f-6ad0d26bca58" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284230 1034 "0\\le x\\le 4+e" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284230 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534365 true] [17592186284230 1111 #uuid "f5193c01-d10c-5900-835d-48ce0b98a7ab" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284231 1017 #edb/ref 17592186284232 13194139534365 true] [17592186284231 1089 "q-102979" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284231 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534365 true] [17592186284231 1100 "Solve the inequality ${log}_{2}⁡(4x-1)\\ge {log}_{2}⁡(1-\\frac{x}{4}).$" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284231 1113 #uuid "9fe7dba8-e1c9-5093-ae4a-b44af700b7b3" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284231 1145 "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $2.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(4x-1)} & \\ge {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(1-x/4)} \\\\ 4x-1 & \\ge 1-\\frac{x}{4} \\\\ \\frac{17x}{4} & \\ge 2 \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{8}{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-1 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{x}{4} & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & <4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\ge \\frac{8}{17},$ which can be expressed as $[\\frac{8}{17},\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x<4,$ which can be expressed as $(\\frac{1}{4},\\infty )$ and $(-\\infty ,4)$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n[\\frac{8}{17},\\infty )\\cap (\\frac{1}{4},\\infty )\\cap (-\\infty ,4)=[\\frac{8}{17},4)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [\\frac{8}{17},4),$ which can also be written as $\\frac{8}{17}\\le x<4.$" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284232 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284233 13194139534365 true] [17592186284232 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284234 13194139534365 true] [17592186284232 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284235 13194139534365 true] [17592186284232 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284236 13194139534365 true] [17592186284232 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284237 13194139534365 true] [17592186284232 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534365 true] [17592186284232 1041 "selection" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284232 1104 #edb/ref 17592186284237 13194139534365 true] [17592186284232 1110 #uuid "a737e259-6b56-586c-8338-68260c22934f" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284233 1034 "x\\ge \\frac{8}{17}" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284233 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534365 true] [17592186284233 1111 #uuid "c599bfdf-f878-533f-9611-490f851505d0" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284234 1034 "0\\le x<\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284234 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534365 true] [17592186284234 1111 #uuid "66116d64-138c-5c2e-b4d3-02b984128a6a" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284235 1034 "\\frac{1}{4}<x<4" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284235 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534365 true] [17592186284235 1111 #uuid "3979e35e-0fa7-5506-92e0-22d710ab9d5f" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284236 1034 "\\frac{1}{4}<x\\le \\frac{8}{17}" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284236 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534365 true] [17592186284236 1111 #uuid "a65334c7-6ba5-5e6c-93a0-b979dff85451" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284237 1034 "\\frac{8}{17}\\le x<4" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284237 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534365 true] [17592186284237 1111 #uuid "ac6b6c25-120d-5563-a6e2-66393b2f535f" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284238 1017 #edb/ref 17592186284239 13194139534365 true] [17592186284238 1017 #edb/ref 17592186284241 13194139534365 true] [17592186284238 1089 "q-278673" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284238 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534365 true] [17592186284238 1100 "Solve the inequality ${log}_{5}⁡(6x-3)\\ge {log}_{5}⁡(3x+9).$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284238 1113 #uuid "eb6f2386-8767-561f-ad3a-bba78785d278" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284238 1145 "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $5.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(6x-3)} & \\ge {5}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x+9)} \\\\ 6x-3 & \\ge 3x+9 \\\\ 3x & \\ge 12 \\\\ x & \\ge 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-3 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 3x+9 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\ge 4,$ which can be expressed as $[4,\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x>-3,$ which can be expressed as $(\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )$ and $(-3,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n[4,\\infty )\\cap (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )\\cap (-3,\\infty )=[4,\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [4,\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $x\\ge 4.$" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284239 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284240 13194139534365 true] [17592186284239 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534365 true] [17592186284239 1041 "field-1" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284239 1104 #edb/ref 17592186284240 13194139534365 true] [17592186284239 1110 #uuid "f13a9a01-5279-53e0-92b6-e691eb6405c9" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284240 1034 "\\ge" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284240 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534365 true] [17592186284240 1111 #uuid "4d372c7d-9ae5-500f-a429-6653130faec5" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284241 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284242 13194139534365 true] [17592186284241 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534365 true] [17592186284241 1041 "field-2" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284241 1104 #edb/ref 17592186284242 13194139534365 true] [17592186284241 1110 #uuid "aadaf901-3c96-5f5d-aa0c-a8b7775ddcdc" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284242 1034 "4" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284242 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534365 true] [17592186284242 1111 #uuid "ea91dc15-097b-59c1-93d1-5304bb4577fd" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284243 1017 #edb/ref 17592186284244 13194139534365 true] [17592186284243 1089 "q-88024" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284243 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534365 true] [17592186284243 1100 "Solve the inequality ${log}_{2}⁡(x+3)+4\\le {log}_{2}⁡(x-2).$" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284243 1113 #uuid "63e61ca1-883d-5b21-b2c1-7fcd88f8bb68" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284243 1145 "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $2.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(x+3)+4} & \\le {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(x-2)} \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(x+3)}\\cdot {2}^{4} & \\le {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(x-2)} \\\\ (x+3)\\cdot 16 & \\le x-2 \\\\ 16x+48 & \\le x-2 \\\\ 15x & \\le -50 \\\\ x & \\le -\\frac{10}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >-3 \\\\ x-2 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le -\\frac{10}{3},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{10}{3}].$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>-3$ and $x>2,$ which can be expressed as $(-3,\\infty )$ and $(2,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-\\frac{10}{3}]\\cap (-3,\\infty )\\cap (2,\\infty )=∅\n$$\n\nThe intersection is empty. Therefore, there is no solution." 13194139534365 true] [17592186284244 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284245 13194139534365 true] [17592186284244 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284246 13194139534365 true] [17592186284244 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284247 13194139534365 true] [17592186284244 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284248 13194139534365 true] [17592186284244 1000 #edb/ref 17592186284249 13194139534365 true] [17592186284244 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534365 true] [17592186284244 1041 "selection" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284244 1104 #edb/ref 17592186284246 13194139534365 true] [17592186284244 1110 #uuid "8efa13e9-8f54-52a8-bd3e-5cb669a7e6c7" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284245 1034 "-3<x\\le \\frac{7}{3}" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284245 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534365 true] [17592186284245 1111 #uuid "327f4ddf-a8a9-59f2-894e-49a8d40e3c0c" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284246 1034 "no solution" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284246 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534365 true] [17592186284246 1111 #uuid "e081b485-b1f9-54f3-9f36-378f7be65630" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284247 1034 "x\\ge \\frac{7}{3}" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284247 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534365 true] [17592186284247 1111 #uuid "a38c851a-4e44-534e-b5f1-5b3d8cbb114b" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284248 1034 "2<x\\le \\frac{8}{3}" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284248 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534365 true] [17592186284248 1111 #uuid "7f9d6572-64a3-5b86-ab42-922026a330cc" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284249 1034 "2<x\\le \\frac{10}{3}" 13194139534365 true] [17592186284249 